<h3>2.2 Protocols and Cipher Suites Analysis</h3>
<p>In modern web infrastructure, public web servers must exclusively support <strong>TLS 1.2</strong> and <strong>TLS 1.3</strong>. Legacy protocols—specifically <strong>SSLv2, SSLv3, TLS 1.0, and TLS 1.1</strong>—suffer from cryptographic vulnerabilities (e.g., POODLE, BEAST) and must be completely disabled at the server or edge load balancer.</p>
<p>Cipher suites must prioritize <strong>AEAD (Authenticated Encryption with Associated Data)</strong> ciphers such as <code>ECDHE-ECDSA-AES128-GCM-SHA256</code> or <code>ECDHE-RSA-AES256-GCM-SHA384</code> with Perfect Forward Secrecy (PFS). All legacy ciphers utilizing RC4, 3DES, or CBC-mode hashing must be removed.</p>

<h3>2.3 Known Cryptographic Vulnerability Assessment</h3>
<p>Standard automated checks were conducted against known TLS vulnerabilities, including Heartbleed (CVE-2014-0160), POODLE (CVE-2014-3566), ROBOT, Ticketbleed, and DROWN. Specific affirmative clearance for [Website Name] remains <span class="badge-pending">Pending Verification</span>.</p>

<h3>2.4 HTTP Strict Transport Security (HSTS)</h3>
<p>HTTP Strict Transport Security (HSTS) is a mission-critical IETF standard (RFC 6797) that instructs web browsers to only communicate over HTTPS, automatically upgrading HTTP requests and refusing connection overrides in the event of certificate errors. For maximum protection, the server must supply:</p>
<pre style="background: #f1f5f9; padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #cbd5e1; font-family: Consolas, monospace;">Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains; preload</pre>
<p>As empirical HSTS header values were not supplied in the input dataset, this configuration is marked as <span class="badge-pending">Pending Verification</span>.</p>

