<h3>3.2 Detailed Analysis of Missing Security Headers</h3>
<p>Where security headers are omitted, the application forfeits native browser-level safeguards. Below is a detailed breakdown of the industry-standard headers that must be validated:</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th style="width: 25%;">Header Name</th>
      <th style="width: 45%;">Functional Mechanism</th>
      <th style="width: 30%;">Security Risk If Missing</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Content-Security-Policy (CSP)</strong></td>
      <td>Restricts valid sources of executable scripts, stylesheets, images, frames, and connections that the browser is permitted to load.</td>
      <td>Leaves the application susceptible to Cross-Site Scripting (XSS), data exfiltration, clickjacking, and malicious code injection.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Strict-Transport-Security (HSTS)</strong></td>
      <td>Enforces HTTPS-only communication and prevents SSL-stripping attacks.</td>
      <td>Man-in-the-Middle (MitM) eavesdropping during initial unencrypted HTTP redirects.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>X-Content-Type-Options</strong></td>
      <td>Explicitly set to <code>nosniff</code>; disables MIME-type sniffing by browsers.</td>
      <td>Permits user-uploaded files (e.g. avatar images) to be executed as malicious JavaScript/HTML.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>X-Frame-Options</strong></td>
      <td>Directs browser whether page can be rendered inside <code>&lt;frame&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;iframe&gt;</code>, or <code>&lt;object&gt;</code> tags. Set to <code>DENY</code> or <code>SAMEORIGIN</code>.</td>
      <td>Permits Clickjacking attacks where invisible overlays trick authenticated users into executing unwanted actions.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Referrer-Policy</strong></td>
      <td>Regulates how much referrer metadata is transmitted when users navigate away. Recommended: <code>strict-origin-when-cross-origin</code>.</td>
      <td>Sensitive query strings, tokens, internal URLs, or private user IDs may leak to third-party endpoints.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Permissions-Policy</strong></td>
      <td>Disables or restricts granular browser hardware APIs (geolocation, microphone, camera, payments, accelerometer).</td>
      <td>Compromised third-party advertising or analytics scripts could silently invoke privileged device APIs.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h3>3.3 Emerging Cross-Origin Isolation Headers</h3>
<p>Modern browser security architectures introduce powerful cross-origin isolation primitives. These headers protect against side-channel attacks (such as Spectre) and cross-origin data leaks:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy (COEP):</strong> When set to <code>require-corp</code>, the document can only load resources that explicitly grant cross-origin permission via CORP or CORS.</li>
  <li><strong>Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy (COOP):</strong> When configured to <code>same-origin</code>, the window is isolated from cross-origin opener windows, preventing cross-window DOM manipulation and Cross-Site Leaks (XS-Leaks).</li>
  <li><strong>Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy (CORP):</strong> Directs browsers to restrict resource reads (images, scripts, JSON) to <code>same-origin</code> or <code>same-site</code>, shielding authenticated assets from unauthorized embedding.</li>
</ul>

<h3>3.4 Information Disclosure & Server Header Leakage</h3>
<p>HTTP response banners frequently leak precise infrastructure details. Standard disclosures include <code>Server: nginx/1.18.0</code>, <code>X-Powered-By: Express</code>, or <code>X-AspNet-Version: 4.0.30319</code>. I evaluated the raw headers for such fingerprinting vectors; current empirical status is <span class="badge-pending">Pending Verification</span>. Disclosing software versions drastically lowers the bar for threat actors seeking unpatched CVE vulnerabilities.</p>

<div class="page-break"></div>

<h1>4. Cookies and Browser Security</h1>

<h3>4.1 Cookie Security Attributes & Explanations</h3>
<p>Client-side cookies are the primary vehicle for maintaining authenticated session state. To prevent credential hijacking, cross-site request forgery, and eavesdropping, every sensitive cookie must be strictly decorated with defensive attributes:</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th style="width: 20%;">Attribute</th>
      <th style="width: 50%;">Technical Mechanism</th>
      <th style="width: 30%;">Required Configuration</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>HttpOnly</strong></td>
      <td>Blocks client-side scripts from reading cookie values via <code>document.cookie</code>. Mitigates session token exfiltration in the event of an XSS flaw.</td>
      <td>Mandatory on all session, authentication, and CSRF tokens.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Secure</strong></td>
      <td>Instructs the browser to only transmit the cookie over encrypted TLS/HTTPS connections. Prevents plaintext transmission over cleartext HTTP.</td>
      <td>Mandatory across all production cookies.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>SameSite</strong></td>
      <td>Controls cookie inclusion during cross-site requests. Mitigates Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF).<br/>
      • <code>Strict</code>: Never sent on cross-site requests.<br/>
      • <code>Lax</code>: Sent on top-level GET navigations (browser default).<br/>
      • <code>None</code>: Sent on all requests; requires <code>Secure</code>.</td>
      <td>Set to <code>SameSite=Lax</code> or <code>SameSite=Strict</code> for authenticated state.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Expires / Max-Age</strong></td>
      <td>Specifies session longevity. Without this, the cookie is an ephemeral session cookie. Excessively long lifespans prolong exposure if endpoints are compromised.</td>
      <td>Enforce reasonable expiration aligning with user risk profiles.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Domain & Path</strong></td>
      <td>Restricts cookie delivery to the narrowest allowable origin and subpath. Broad domain scoping (e.g. <code>.example.com</code>) exposes cookies to sibling subdomains.</td>
      <td>Omit <code>Domain</code> attribute to default to host-only scope; restrict <code>Path</code>.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

