<h1>7. Findings and Risk Assessment</h1>

<h3>7.1 Risk Level Definitions</h3>
<p>To ensure rigorous objectivity, all vulnerabilities and architectural defects are classified in accordance with standardized risk criteria:</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th style="width: 25%;">Risk Classification</th>
      <th style="width: 75%;">Definition and Threat Impact</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><span class="badge-critical">Critical</span></td>
      <td>Severe, immediately exploitable vulnerability enabling remote code execution, unauthenticated database exfiltration, or complete infrastructure takeover. Requires immediate emergency remediation.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><span class="badge-high">High</span></td>
      <td>Significant security flaw that permits account takeover, privilege escalation, or widespread data tampering without requiring specialized prerequisites.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><span class="badge-medium">Medium</span></td>
      <td>Defect in defensive controls (e.g., missing essential security headers, weak cookie flags) that substantially increases attack surface or facilitates secondary exploits.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><span class="badge-low">Low</span></td>
      <td>Minor hygiene issue or informational disclosure with minimal direct exploitability (e.g., verbose server banners).</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><span class="badge-info">Informational</span></td>
      <td>Architecture, accessibility, or performance observation aligned with industry best practices without direct vulnerability exposure.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><span class="badge-pending">Pending Verification</span></td>
      <td>Identified scope area where empirical scan results were not provided in the testing brief and must be validated through active live data.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><span class="badge-info">Not Assessed</span></td>
      <td>Component deliberately excluded from the current passive scope or inaccessible during passive testing.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h3>7.2 Detailed Findings Registry</h3>

<div style="border: 1px solid #cbd5e1; padding: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; background: #ffffff;">
  <p style="margin: 0 0 6px 0;"><strong>Finding ID:</strong> <code>F-01</code> | <strong>Category:</strong> Security Headers | <strong>Risk Level:</strong> <span class="badge-medium">Medium (Pending Verification)</span></p>
  <p><strong>Description:</strong> The presence and enforcement of baseline defensive HTTP security headers—specifically Content-Security-Policy (CSP), Strict-Transport-Security (HSTS), X-Content-Type-Options, and X-Frame-Options—have not been empirically verified in the supplied test data.</p>
  <p><strong>Potential Impact:</strong> Failure to configure these headers leaves the client browser without authoritative rules to block cross-site scripting (XSS), clickjacking frame embedding, and protocol downgrade attacks.</p>
  <p><strong>Recommendation:</strong> Deploy a strict CSP, set HSTS with a one-year max-age, enforce <code>X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff</code>, and restrict framing via CSP <code>frame-ancestors</code>.</p>
</div>

<div style="border: 1px solid #cbd5e1; padding: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; background: #ffffff;">
  <p style="margin: 0 0 6px 0;"><strong>Finding ID:</strong> <code>F-02</code> | <strong>Category:</strong> Transport Layer Security | <strong>Risk Level:</strong> <span class="badge-medium">Medium (Pending Verification)</span></p>
  <p><strong>Description:</strong> Qualys SSL Labs rating, certificate cipher suites, and HSTS preloading status remain unconfirmed in the submitted test dataset.</p>
  <p><strong>Potential Impact:</strong> If the target web server supports obsolete TLS protocols (TLS 1.0/1.1) or weak cipher suites, users connecting over untrusted Wi-Fi networks could be subject to passive eavesdropping or man-in-the-middle decryption.</p>
  <p><strong>Recommendation:</strong> Restrict cipher negotiation to TLS 1.2 and TLS 1.3 exclusively; verify certificate renewal pipelines and submit the domain to the Chrome HSTS preload list.</p>
</div>

<div style="border: 1px solid #cbd5e1; padding: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; background: #ffffff;">
  <p style="margin: 0 0 6px 0;"><strong>Finding ID:</strong> <code>F-03</code> | <strong>Category:</strong> Client State & Cookies | <strong>Risk Level:</strong> <span class="badge-medium">Medium (Pending Verification)</span></p>
  <p><strong>Description:</strong> Cookie attributes (HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite) and web storage usage (LocalStorage vs. Cookies) were submitted with placeholder designations and require empirical verification.</p>
  <p><strong>Potential Impact:</strong> Absence of the <code>HttpOnly</code> attribute allows malicious scripts to harvest session cookies via document.cookie. Absence of <code>SameSite=Lax/Strict</code> permits CSRF attacks on authenticated endpoints.</p>
  <p><strong>Recommendation:</strong> Enforce <code>HttpOnly; Secure; SameSite=Lax</code> on all session tokens. Ensure sensitive tokens are never stored in browser <code>localStorage</code>.</p>
</div>

<div style="border: 1px solid #cbd5e1; padding: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; background: #ffffff;">
  <p style="margin: 0 0 6px 0;"><strong>Finding ID:</strong> <code>F-04</code> | <strong>Category:</strong> Information Disclosure | <strong>Risk Level:</strong> <span class="badge-low">Low (Pending Verification)</span></p>
  <p><strong>Description:</strong> Server banners (e.g. <code>Server</code>, <code>X-Powered-By</code>) have not been confirmed as stripped from response payloads.</p>
  <p><strong>Potential Impact:</strong> Exposing exact web server and framework runtime versions facilitates automated recon and vulnerability targeting by opportunistic attackers.</p>
  <p><strong>Recommendation:</strong> Strip <code>Server</code>, <code>X-Powered-By</code>, and related metadata headers at the reverse proxy or web application server level.</p>
</div>

<div style="border: 1px solid #cbd5e1; padding: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; background: #ffffff;">
  <p style="margin: 0 0 6px 0;"><strong>Finding ID:</strong> <code>F-05</code> | <strong>Category:</strong> Web Performance & Vitals | <strong>Risk Level:</strong> <span class="badge-info">Informational (Pending Verification)</span></p>
  <p><strong>Description:</strong> Core Web Vitals (FCP, LCP, TBT, CLS) and Google Lighthouse performance metrics require validation against live production traffic.</p>
  <p><strong>Potential Impact:</strong> Sub-optimal loading speeds, heavy JavaScript blocking, and visual shifts degrade user retention, increase bounce rates, and hurt organic search ranking.</p>
  <p><strong>Recommendation:</strong> Implement responsive next-gen images (AVIF/WebP), optimize critical rendering path, eliminate unused JavaScript, and adhere to Core Web Vitals thresholds.</p>
</div>

<div style="border: 1px solid #cbd5e1; padding: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; background: #ffffff;">
  <p style="margin: 0 0 6px 0;"><strong>Finding ID:</strong> <code>F-06</code> | <strong>Category:</strong> Agentic Browsing Readiness | <strong>Risk Level:</strong> <span class="badge-info">Informational (Pending Verification)</span></p>
  <p><strong>Description:</strong> Emerging autonomous AI agents and automated browsers require machine-readable semantic structures (Schema.org, JSON-LD, ARIA roles, llms.txt, clear robots.txt directives) to navigate web resources reliably.</p>
  <p><strong>Potential Impact:</strong> Lack of semantic machine readability inhibits next-generation AI search visibility, multimodal assistant integration, and automated workflow processing.</p>
  <p><strong>Recommendation:</strong> Implement structured data (JSON-LD), establish clean semantic HTML hierarchies, and publish an <code>llms.txt</code> file to guide autonomous agentic interactions.</p>
</div>

